<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A ship is at <m>P({5}^\circ\,\mathrm{N}, {170}^\circ\,\mathrm{E})</m>.</p><p>(a) It sails due south along its meridian through <m>{12}^\circ</m> of latitude. Give its new latitude.</p><p><fillin characters="4" name="ans1"/> <m>^\circ</m> [ N | S ]  </p><p>(b) Instead, it sails due east along its parallel through <m>{25}^\circ</m> of longitude. Give its new longitude.</p><p><fillin characters="4" name="ans3"/> <m>^\circ</m> [ E | W ]  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Count along the meridian, with the equator as <m>0^\circ</m>. The first <m>{5}^\circ</m> take the ship to the equator; the remaining <m>{7}^\circ</m> take it into the other hemisphere. New latitude: <m>{7}^\circ\,\mathrm{S}</m>.</p><p>(b) Think of longitude as a number line from <m>180^\circ\,\mathrm{W}</m> through <m>0^\circ</m> to <m>180^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m>, joined up at <m>180^\circ</m>. The ship passes the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian: counting on gives <m>{195}^\circ</m>, which is <m>360^\circ - {195}^\circ = {165}^\circ</m> on the other side. New longitude: <m>{165}^\circ\,\mathrm{W}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>7</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p>S</p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>165</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p>W</p></answer>
</stack-static>